  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level disturbance will be passing by to the north\nacross southern Canada on Thursday. At the surface, a weak\nsurface trough is expected to pass through the area from west to\neast. With a low-level southerly flow in place, it will be\nnoticeably more humid than recent days, with dewpoints reaching\nthe mid to upper 60s. Once a warm front passes through the area\nearly in the day, instability will be increasing. 00z HRRR shows\nSBCAPE values will reach 1000-2000 J/kg and the 00z SPC HREF\nalso shows mean values exceeding 1000 J/kg in valley areas as\nwell. With the disturbance passing by to the north, there should\nbe some shear in place as well, with 0-6 km bulk shear exceeding\n30 kts.\n\nAlthough the day will start out dry, scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to develop during the afternoon hours\nand continue into the evening. With enough instability and shear\nin place, some of the storms may become severe, with the main\nthreat being damaging winds. There could be enough helicity for\nan isolated tornado, but damaging straight-line winds looks to\nbe the most likely threat. CAMs suggest that storms may organize\ninto short lines or bow echoes, especially by the evening hours.\nStorms may linger into the late-evening, but should dissipate\ntowards midnight, as the surface trough pushes eastward and the\nloss of daytime heating reduces the available instability. SPC \nhas a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for parts of the area (Mohawk \nValley and eastern Catskills), with a marginal risk (level 1 of \n5) for the remainder of the area. \n\nIn addition, there will be the threat for heavy downpours and \nisolated flash flooding, thanks to the high moisture in place \n(PWATS reaching 1.50 to 2.00\", which is about 1-2 STD above \nnormal). This allow for high intensity rainfall rates within any\nthunderstorm. Urban and poor drainage areas will be most as \nrisk, as well as locations that see multiple thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe actual cold front will be crossing the region during the day\non Friday. A few additional showers or thunderstorms can't be\nruled out on Friday ahead of the front, although the threat for\nstronger storms looks lower on Friday due to less instability\nand shear, and the front passing earlier in the day.\n\nBehind the front, it will turn less humid for the weekend,\nespecially on Saturday when dewpoints will mainly be in the 50s.\nWith high pressure in control, there should be plenty of sun\nboth days with no threat for precipitation. Temperatures will \nbe fairly seasonable, with daytime temperatures reaching the \nupper 70s to low 80s and overnight lows in the 50s, with even \nsome 40s across the high terrain.",
